{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1493328-questions-about-three-oblivion-books","id":1493328,"title":"Questions about three Oblivion books","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-09-19T07:44:40-07:00","id":1493328,"title":"Questions about three Oblivion books","content":"I see from the wiki that three books delve mainly with the deepest part of the setting:<br /> - 1995 The Sea of Shadows<br /> - 1998 Doomslayers<br /> - 2019 Book of Oblivion<br /> <br /> Is see, always from the wiki, that some of the chapters seems to overlap. Of course they came from three different editions so the overlapping is fully understandable.<br /> Which are their main differences? Which are your favourite and why? Which do you suggest to read first, and why?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26404-submariner93","name":"Submariner93","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26404_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-19T10:09:43-07:00","id":1493338,"title":"","content":"Don't forget the Dark Reflections Spectres and Shadow Players Guide<br /> <br /> Sea of Shadows is a first edition book and it reads like one. I don't have any specific examples since I haven't read the book in years. I do remember it being fairly short, probably because it included an adventure. I think it involved a trip through the labyrinth as a shortcut but don't quote me on that.<br /> <br /> Doomslayers is quite possibly my favorite WW supplement. It is largely concerned with those specific Wraiths crazy enough to dedicate their afterlives to hunting Spectres. It also has a hefty section on the Spectral hierarchy. <br /> <br /> The book of Oblivion is a more general sourcebook which includes direct information on playing and running Shadows and Spectres (much like Dark Reflections) but also details on the Tempest and the Dark Kingdoms.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-19T17:37:11-07:00","id":1493370,"title":"","content":"I don't have the 2019 Book of Oblivion and cannot comment. But I do own both Sea of Shadows and Doomslayers. I think both are high quality sourcebooks for Wraith.<br /> <br /> The Sea of Shadows is primarily about the Tempest and how to use it in your Wraith chronicle. So it includes information on the various types of creatures who dwell there, hazards that can be encountered, and other information an ST would want to have to run any adventures that require the PCs to spend a lot of time in the Tempest. It also has a section on running Harrowings. The adventure in it is not about going to the Labyrinth, but travelling to one of the minor Far Shores.<br /> <br /> Doomslayers contains almost everything you need if you want to run a chronicle about the PCs fighting Spectres (but not everything, you need Dark Reflections: Spectres as well). So it covers the organizations and resources the Hierarchy has to discover and destroy Spectres. It has a lot of information about running adventures in the Labyrinth. And it contains a lot of information about Spectres, their castes, factions, etc.<br /> <br /> I don't think there is much overlap between the two. Although obviously if you want to run an adventures where the PCs must travel through the Tempest before arriving at the Labyrinth, you want both.<br /> <br /> I think Wraith has the best quality sourcebooks out of all the original WoD game lines. There is a lot of meat, and very little fat. And given the scope of the Wraith game, it has a lot of useful information as opposed to trivia or soap opera in the guise of lore. I rate both sourcebooks fairly highly, but think Doomslayers is the stronger of the two (possibly because it has a much higher page count - SoS is around 100 pages, DS is around 170).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}